## Solution

We compute
$$I=\int_{0}^{2} \frac{4 \sqrt{2-x}-\sqrt{x+2}}{(\sqrt{x+2}+4 \sqrt{2-x})(x+2)^{2}}\,dx.$$

**Substitution.** Set $x=2\cos\theta$, so $dx=-2\sin\theta\,d\theta$, with $\theta:\pi/2\to 0$ as $x:0\to 2$. Then
$$2-x=2(1-\cos\theta)=4\sin^{2}\!\tfrac{\theta}{2},\qquad x+2=2(1+\cos\theta)=4\cos^{2}\!\tfrac{\theta}{2},$$
hence $\sqrt{2-x}=2\sin\phi$, $\sqrt{x+2}=2\cos\phi$ where $\phi=\theta/2$, and $(x+2)^{2}=16\cos^{4}\phi$.

Since $dx=-4\sin\theta\,d\theta=-8\sin\phi\cos\phi\,d\phi$, the limits become $\phi:\pi/4\to 0$. The numerator and the linear factor in the denominator are
$$4\sqrt{2-x}-\sqrt{x+2}=8\sin\phi-2\cos\phi,\qquad \sqrt{x+2}+4\sqrt{2-x}=2\cos\phi+8\sin\phi.$$
Thus
$$I=\int_{\pi/4}^{0}\frac{(8\sin\phi-2\cos\phi)(-8\sin\phi\cos\phi)}{(2\cos\phi+8\sin\phi)\,16\cos^{4}\phi}\,d\phi
=\frac12\int_{0}^{\pi/4}\frac{(4\sin\phi-\cos\phi)\sin\phi}{(\cos\phi+4\sin\phi)\cos^{3}\phi}\,d\phi.$$

**Second substitution.** Let $t=\tan\phi$, $d\phi=\frac{dt}{1+t^{2}}$, with $t:0\to 1$. Using $\sin\phi=\frac{t}{\sqrt{1+t^{2}}}$, $\cos\phi=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1+t^{2}}}$:
$$\frac{(4\sin\phi-\cos\phi)\sin\phi}{(\cos\phi+4\sin\phi)\cos^{3}\phi}\,d\phi
=\frac{(4t-1)\,t}{1+4t}\,dt.$$
Therefore
$$I=\frac12\int_{0}^{1}\frac{(4t-1)t}{1+4t}\,dt=\frac12\int_{0}^{1}\frac{4t^{2}-t}{4t+1}\,dt.$$

**Polynomial division.**
$$\frac{4t^{2}-t}{4t+1}=t-\frac12+\frac{1}{2(4t+1)}.$$
Hence
$$\int_{0}^{1}\!\left(t-\frac12+\frac{1}{2(4t+1)}\right)dt
=\left[\frac{t^{2}}{2}-\frac{t}{2}+\frac18\ln(4t+1)\right]_{0}^{1}
=\frac12-\frac12+\frac18\ln 5=\frac18\ln 5.$$

**Result.**
$$I=\frac12\cdot\frac18\ln 5=\boxed{\dfrac{\ln 5}{16}}.$$

A numerical check (Simpson's rule, $n=8$) gives $\approx 0.0984$, consistent with $\frac{\ln 5}{16}\approx 0.10059$ and converging toward it.

